<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Akwamu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Akwamu was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> state in the forest and coastal region of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> that was powerful in the seventeenth and early eighteenth centuries. It controlled trade routes to the coast and was a major regional rival. Its decline and the wider competition among Akan states formed part of the background to the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to Akwamu oral tradition, the royal Abrade clan once ruled the Kingdom of Twifo, an early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> state in the Twifo-Hemang area south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pra-river">Pra River</a>. After the death of its fifth ruler a succession dispute split the ruling lineage, and a section under Otomfo Asare broke away to found a new dynasty at Asamankese. The earliest written record of Akwamu is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> map of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> dated 1629, which called it a predatory nation; the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivor-wilks">Ivor Wilks</a> read this as a reference to its strict control of merchants on the routes through its territory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1677 the Akwamuhene Ansa Sasraku I attacked the Ga kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a>, destroyed its inland capital and executed its king, Okai Koi, with his heir. In 1679 Akwamu armies overran the Adangme kingdom of Ladoku, and between 1680 and 1681 the remaining Ga towns, Small Accra, Osu and Labadi, were defeated and annexed. Accra remained an Akwamu province for the next fifty years. Akwamu tradition holds that Ansa Sasraku sheltered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osei-tutu-i">Osei Tutu</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a> when he fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denkyira">Denkyira</a> overlordship, and helped him return to power.</p><p>In 1693 Akwamu forces seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osu-castle">Christiansborg Castle</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> and held it for about a year. Under Nana Ado they crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volta-river">Volta River</a>, taking Little Popo in April 1702 and Whydah in May; Whydah stayed tributary for over two decades. His successor Akonno, from 1703, campaigned against Krepi and Kwahu, and by 1710 the empire reached from Agona in the west to the borders of Whydah. Civil war weakened the state in the 1720s, and in 1730 a coalition destroyed the capital, Nyanaoase. Akyem forces annexed the heartland, and the surviving leaders formed a smaller state at Akwamufie, east of the Volta.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Akwamuhene ruled as first among equals with senior officials called abirempon, who served as advisers, army commanders, heads of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> and members of the state council, and who governed distant coastal towns and inland districts on his behalf. Around Nyanaoase lay royal and noble plantations worked by war captives. The army was built around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musketeers</a>, and by the early eighteenth century the king kept several thousand hereditary slave-soldiers known as sika den. Akwamu taxed and fined the European forts, and by the late seventeenth century its exports shifted from gold toward enslaved people. Akwamu captives taken to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> were involved in the 1733 St. John revolt.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Akwamu Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>150 enslaved people from <b>Akwamu</b>, in present-day Ghana, revolted against the owners and managers of the plantations on Sankt Jan (St. John) in the Danish West Indies.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-23">23 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1733, after Akyem Abuakwa and its allies broke <b>Akwamu</b> power, the ridge towns accepted an Akyem leader, Safori, as their ruler.</blockquote><a href="/state/akuapem">Akuapem</a></li></ul></section>
